= Ideation <sec-ideation>

== Initial ideas on the table and why they were considered

Two main directions were on the table. The first was our earliest concept: a blockchain-based model, using Graph Neural Networks, to monitor and analyze cryptocurrency transactions for compliance with EU transparency regulations, making ownership and transaction data verifiable and tamper-proof, with a roadmap to later extend to traditional bank transactions. It was considered because initial market research indicated a clear theoretical need for advanced transaction monitoring given the scale of illicit crypto activity, and early signals suggested potential clients existed within this ecosystem.

The second direction, which became ComplAI, was an AI-native diagnostic tool designed to help early-stage FinTechs and SMEs navigate complex European regulation. This idea entered our consideration set once discovery conversations kept surfacing the same complaint from founders across very different products: regulatory compliance, not the underlying technology, was what consumed their time and capital before launch.

== Selection criteria and method used to narrow down

To evaluate both ideas, we applied a feasibility and market barrier assessment grounded in direct customer discovery and expert interviews. At each stage, we tested whether an idea could realistically be built and sold by a lean, early-stage team within a reasonable timeframe, letting practitioner feedback override our own enthusiasm.

Applied to the crypto monitoring idea, this process revealed critical flaws. Speaking with domain experts, including Niklas Ego at the Deutsche Bundesbank, made clear that institutional transaction monitoring is already dominated by established players with millions in funding and already made deals, making it very difficult for a new entrant to reach the operational bar these clients expect. More importantly, direct interviews surfaced a harder truth about buyer incentives: traditional banks do not prioritize tracking illicit funds proactively unless facing immediate risk of sanction. The internal need to use better detection technology was not reachable by the capabilities of an early stage startup.

Applied to the ComplAI concept, the same filter produced a clearly different result. Interviews with founders, former bankers, and legal experts confirmed a clear pain point and a real willingness to pay. Furthermore, building a diagnostic tool required a much lower barrier to entry than developing a real-time institutional monitoring system.

== Why this idea was chosen over the alternatives

We chose to pursue ComplAI over the original crypto concept for several concrete reasons. First, the pain point was massive and independently validated well beyond our own research: European regulatory compliance is difficult even for large, well-resourced companies: this can be easily exemplified by the significant GDPR fine levied against Google by French regulators and the regulatory pressure that pushed Binance out of parts of the European market. Second, the status quo is genuinely cost prohibitive for our target segment.

For early-stage FinTechs, legal and compliance spend is often the single largest expense, and unlike larger corporations, these startups lack the capital for internal legal teams, turning a manageable cost for an incumbent into an existential threat for a founder. Third, this pain point was directly confirmed through our own market validation, and the barrier to entry proved significantly lower than competing against entrenched institutional vendors in transaction monitoring.

Together, these factors made the shift from a technical, institutionally-facing idea to a founder-facing diagnostic platform the clear strategic choice.
